.TH CTPOOL_WAIT 3 "2026-08-02" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ctpool_wait \- block until the pool is idle
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <cthreadpool.h>

void ctpool_wait(ctpool pool);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Blocks until the task queue is empty and all active tasks have
finished executing. Does not shut down the pool; worker threads
remain alive and new tasks may be submitted after this call returns.

Contract: no other thread should be submitting tasks concurrently
when ctpool_wait is called. With an unbounded queue, concurrent
submissions can cause indefinite blocking.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
pool   Thread pool handle (CTPOOL_INVALID or a stale/already-destroyed handle is a no-op)
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
A pool inherited across a fork(2) call (its worker threads are not
duplicated into the child) is treated as vacuously idle: ctpool_wait
on such a pool in the child returns immediately rather than waiting
for progress no worker thread in that process can ever make.

Calling ctpool_wait on pool from within a task (or that task's
on_complete callback) currently executing on one of pool's own
worker threads is also treated as vacuously idle and returns
immediately: the calling task is itself still counted among pool's
active tasks until it returns, so waiting here would otherwise
deadlock the calling worker against itself.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ctpool_shutdown_drain (3),
.BR ctpool_shutdown_immediate (3)
